    "knowledge_point_id": "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding a Single Point of Intersection of a Circle with a Line",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/91ead412d3e333fd4bfbcce785813a530e66ca39858ca108e9920d349443bb53.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point where the line $-2x+3y=12$ intersects the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13$ (sketched above).",
    "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation $-2x+3y=12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x}{3}+4\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{2x}{3}+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+4+1)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+5)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{3}+25 & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{26x}{3}+13 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
